Options II, Honour School of Mathematics Part A, Trinity Term; Thursday 30 June 2011, 9.30am–12.30pm. The complete sixteen-page source was supplied through https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/maths_past_papers_3[the past-papers disclosure], in original Part A bundle pages 282–297. The private editable transcription retains the complete cover and every printed question.

New solutions cover the eighteen questions without an exact worked-answer advertisement found in the bounded availability check. H2 is recorded separately because Oxford explicitly advertises solutions for its students in https://users.ox.ac.uk/~math0391/CMrevision.pdf[the Classical Mechanics revision notes]. No new solution is supplied for that question.

= A
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Introduction to Fields}

= A2
{parent=2011/A}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/A/A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/A/A2/a}

A <splitting field> of $f$ over $F$ is a <field extension> $K/F$ in which $f$ is a product of linear factors and which is generated over $F$ by its roots. The generation condition rules out adjoining unrelated elements.

For existence, induct on the <degree of a polynomial>. Choose an <irreducible polynomial> divisor $p$ of $f$. The <quotient ring> $F[X]/(p)$ is a <field> because $(p)$ is a <maximal ideal>; the class $\alpha$ of $X$ is a <root> of $p$, hence of $f$. Over this <field extension>, divide out $X-\alpha$ and apply induction to the remaining <polynomial>. This gives an extension in which $f$ splits. Inside it, the <subfield> generated by all roots is a <splitting field>. The induction starts with a linear <polynomial>, whose <splitting field> is already $F$. Thus **every positive-degree <polynomial> has a splitting <field>**; being monic is unnecessary for existence.

= b
{parent=2011/A/A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/A/A2/b}

The cubic has no <root> in $\mathbb F_2$, since its values at $0$ and $1$ are both $1$. A cubic over a <field> is <irreducible polynomial>[irreducible] precisely when it has no <root>. Consequently
$$
K=\mathbb F_2[\alpha],\qquad \alpha^3+\alpha+1=0,
$$
is a <field extension> of <degree of a field extension>[degree] $3$, with eight elements. The <Frobenius endomorphism> $x\mapsto x^2$ takes a <root> of a <polynomial> over $\mathbb F_2$ to another <root>, so the roots are $\alpha,\alpha^2,\alpha^4$. They are distinct: equality between any two would make the <Frobenius endomorphism> orbit have length less than $3$, contradicting the <minimal polynomial> of $\alpha$. They all lie in $K$, and one of them generates $K$. Hence
$$
\boxed{K\cong\mathbb F_8,\qquad [K:\mathbb F_2]=3.}
$$

= c
{parent=2011/A/A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/A/A2/c}

The roots are $\pm\sqrt2$ and $\pm\sqrt3$, so the <splitting field> is $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt3)$. The first adjunction has <degree of a field extension>[degree] $2$. To see that the second does too, suppose $\sqrt3=u+v\sqrt2$ with $u,v\in\mathbb Q$. Squaring and comparing rational and irrational parts gives $uv=0$ and $u^2+2v^2=3$. If $v=0$, then $u^2=3$; if $u=0$, then $v^2=3/2$. Neither is a rational square, as follows by comparing prime exponents in numerator and denominator. Thus $\sqrt3\notin\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)$. The <tower law> now yields
$$
\boxed{[K:\mathbb Q]=4,\qquad K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt3).}
$$
A <basis> is $1,\sqrt2,\sqrt3,\sqrt6$.

= B
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Group Theory}

= B2
{parent=2011/B}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/B/B2/a}

A <composition series> is a finite chain
$$
1=G_0\triangleleft G_1\triangleleft\cdots\triangleleft G_r=G
$$
whose inclusions are strict and whose <quotient group>[quotients] $G_i/G_{i-1}$ are <simple group>[simple]. These <quotient group>[quotients] are the <composition factor>[composition factors]. The <normal subgroup> condition is relative to the next member, rather than necessarily to all of $G$.

Induct on the order of a <finite group>. The trivial <group> has the empty <composition series>. Otherwise choose a <maximal normal subgroup> $N\lneq G$, which exists because there are finitely many subgroups. The <subgroup correspondence theorem> identifies <normal subgroup>[normal subgroups] of $G/N$ with <normal subgroup>[normal subgroups] of $G$ containing $N$, so maximality makes $G/N$ a <simple group>. By induction $N$ has a <composition series>; appending $G$ gives one for $G$.

For example, the nonabelian <symmetric group> $S_3$ has
$$
1\triangleleft A_3\triangleleft S_3,
$$
with <composition factor>[composition factors] $C_3$ and $C_2$, both <abelian group>[abelian].

= b
{parent=2011/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/B/B2/b}

In the <Klein four-group> $V=\{1,a,b,ab\}$, the chains
$$
1\triangleleft\langle a\rangle\triangleleft V,
\qquad
1\triangleleft\langle b\rangle\triangleleft V
$$
are distinct <composition series>. All their <composition factor>[composition factors] are $C_2$.

The <Jordan–Hölder theorem> states that any two <composition series> of a <group> have the same length and the same <composition factor>[composition factors] up to permutation and <group isomorphism>. Thus the <subgroup> chains may differ, while the multiset of simple <quotient group>[quotients] is intrinsic.

= c
{parent=2011/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/B/B2/c}

Write $C_n=\langle g\rangle$ and set $N=\langle g^p\rangle$. The <order of a group element>[order] of $g^p$ is $n/\gcd(n,p)=m$, so $N\cong C_m$. Every <subgroup> of an <abelian group> is a <normal subgroup>. The <quotient group> $C_n/N$ is generated by $gN$ and has order $n/m=p$, hence is $C_p$ and is a <simple group>. By the <subgroup correspondence theorem>, there is no proper <normal subgroup> strictly between $N$ and $C_n$. Therefore **$N$ is a maximal <normal subgroup>** with the required <quotient group>.

= d
{parent=2011/B/B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/B/B2/d}

Suppose
$$
n=p_1\cdots p_r=q_1\cdots q_s
$$
are two factorizations into primes. Repeatedly apply the cyclic-<subgroup> construction of part (c), first removing $p_r$, then $p_{r-1}$, and so on. This constructs a <composition series> for $C_n$ whose <composition factor>[composition factors] are $C_{p_1},\ldots,C_{p_r}$. The second factorization constructs another <composition series> with <composition factor>[composition factors] $C_{q_1},\ldots,C_{q_s}$.

By the <Jordan–Hölder theorem>, $r=s$ and these <composition factor>[composition factors] agree up to <group isomorphism> and permutation. Since isomorphic <finite group>[finite groups] have the same order, the corresponding primes agree. This proves **uniqueness of <prime factorization> up to permutation**, using existence of prime factorizations but not their uniqueness.

= C
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Number Theory}

= C2
{parent=2011/C}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/C/C2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/C/C2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/a/i}

For an odd <prime number> $p$, a nonzero class $a\pmod p$ is a <quadratic residue> if $a\equiv x^2\pmod p$ for some <integer> $x$ not divisible by $p$. The <Legendre symbol> is
$$
\left(\frac ap\right)=
\begin{cases}
0,&p\mid a,\\
1,&a\text{ is a nonzero square modulo }p,\\
-1,&a\text{ is a nonsquare modulo }p.
\end{cases}
$$
When discussing solvability of $x^2\equiv a\pmod p$ without a coprimality assumption, zero is also a square. This convention matters in the final local characterization of <integer> squares.

= ii
{parent=2011/C/C2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/a/ii}

The <Euler criterion> states that for an odd <prime number> $p$ and $p\nmid a$,
$$
\boxed{a^{(p-1)/2}\equiv\left(\frac ap\right)\pmod p.}
$$
The same <modular congruence> remains true when $p\mid a$, with both sides zero.

= iii
{parent=2011/C/C2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/a/iii}

For distinct odd <prime number>[primes] $p$ and $q$, <quadratic reciprocity> states
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac pq\right)\left(\frac qp\right)=(-1)^{(p-1)(q-1)/4}.}
$$
The supplementary laws are
$$
\left(\frac{-1}p\right)=(-1)^{(p-1)/2},\qquad
\left(\frac2p\right)=(-1)^{(p^2-1)/8}.
$$

= iv
{parent=2011/C/C2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/a/iv}

If $p$ divides $a$ or $b$, both sides of the claimed <multiplicativity> identity are zero. Otherwise the <Euler criterion> gives
$$
\left(\frac{ab}p\right)\equiv (ab)^{(p-1)/2}
\equiv a^{(p-1)/2}b^{(p-1)/2}
\equiv\left(\frac ap\right)\left(\frac bp\right)\pmod p.
$$
Each side is $1$ or $-1$. Since $p$ is odd, these two integers represent different residue classes. Hence the <modular congruence> gives equality:
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{ab}p\right)=\left(\frac ap\right)\left(\frac bp\right).}
$$

= b
{parent=2011/C/C2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/C/C2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/b/i}

For odd $m,n$,
$$
\frac{mn-1}2-\frac{m-1}2-\frac{n-1}2
=\frac{(m-1)(n-1)}2
$$
is even. Induction over the prime factors of $b$, repeated according to multiplicity, therefore gives
$$
\frac{b-1}2\equiv\sum_{j=1}^r\frac{p_j-1}2\pmod2.
$$
Using the supplementary law for each <Legendre symbol> in the definition of the <Jacobi symbol>,
$$
\left(\frac{-1}b\right)=\prod_j(-1)^{(p_j-1)/2}
=\boxed{(-1)^{(b-1)/2}}.
$$
For $b=1$, the empty product and the displayed expression are both $1$.

= ii
{parent=2011/C/C2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/b/ii}

If $\gcd(b,c)>1$, both <Jacobi symbol>[Jacobi symbols] vanish, so the formula holds. Otherwise write $b=\prod_i p_i$ and $c=\prod_j q_j$, retaining repeated factors. <Multiplicativity of the Jacobi symbol> and <quadratic reciprocity> give
$$
\left(\frac bc\right)
=\prod_{i,j}\left(\frac{p_i}{q_j}\right)
=\prod_{i,j}\left(\frac{q_j}{p_i}\right)
(-1)^{\sum_{i,j}(p_i-1)(q_j-1)/4}.
$$
The exponent is
$$
\left(\sum_i\frac{p_i-1}2\right)
\left(\sum_j\frac{q_j-1}2\right)
\equiv\frac{b-1}2\frac{c-1}2\pmod2
$$
by the parity identity from part (i). The product of the reversed <Legendre symbol>[Legendre symbols] is $(c/b)$. Thus the <Jacobi reciprocity law> is
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac bc\right)=\left(\frac cb\right)(-1)^{(b-1)(c-1)/4}.}
$$
This also covers $b=1$ or $c=1$ by empty products.

= c
{parent=2011/C/C2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/C/C2/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/c/i}

Here $b$ is odd and nonzero. Choose $k=4|b|-1$. This is a positive odd <integer>, $k\equiv3\pmod4$, and $\gcd(k,b)=1$. By <multiplicativity of the Jacobi symbol>,
$$
\left(\frac ak\right)=\left(\frac{-1}k\right)\left(\frac bk\right)^2
=(-1)^{(k-1)/2}\cdot1=\boxed{-1}.
$$
The coprimality condition ensures that the squared <Jacobi symbol> is $1$ rather than $0$.

= ii
{parent=2011/C/C2/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/c/ii}

Choose a nonzero <quadratic nonresidue> $c\pmod q$. There is one because the squaring map on $\mathbb F_q^\times$ is two-to-one, so exactly half its elements are squares. The moduli $4,q,b$ are pairwise <coprime>, so the <Chinese remainder theorem> gives a positive <integer> $k$ satisfying
$$
k\equiv1\pmod4,\qquad k\equiv c\pmod q,\qquad k\equiv1\pmod b.
$$
When $b=1$, the last condition is vacuous. In particular $k$ is odd and <coprime> to $qb$. Since $k\equiv1\pmod4$, <Jacobi reciprocity> gives
$$
\left(\frac qk\right)=\left(\frac kq\right)=-1,
\qquad
\left(\frac bk\right)=\left(\frac kb\right)=1,
\qquad
\left(\frac{-1}k\right)=1.
$$
As $t$ is odd, <multiplicativity of the Jacobi symbol> now yields
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac ak\right)=\left(\frac{\pm1}k\right)
\left(\frac qk\right)^t\left(\frac bk\right)=-1.}
$$

= iii
{parent=2011/C/C2/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/C/C2/c/iii}

Every <integer> square remains a square modulo every <prime number>, including the zero class at its prime divisors. Conversely, let $a$ be an odd <integer> which is not an <integer> square. If $a$ is the negative of a square, part (i) supplies an odd $k$ with $(a/k)=-1$. In every other case, the <prime factorization> of $|a|$ has an odd exponent $t$ at some odd prime $q$. Write $a=\pm q^t b$ with $b>0$ odd and $\gcd(q,b)=1$, and apply part (ii).

In either case, factor the resulting $k$ into primes. Since its <Jacobi symbol> is a product of <Legendre symbol>[Legendre symbols] and equals $-1$, at least one prime factor $p$ has $(a/p)=-1$. Thus $a$ is not a square modulo that prime. This proves **an odd <integer> is an <integer> square exactly when it is a square modulo every prime**. If the phrase <quadratic residue> is restricted to nonzero residue classes, the final statement must instead say “a square modulo every prime”; for example, $9$ reduces to zero modulo $3$.

= D
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Integration}

= D3
{parent=2011/D}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/D/D3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/D/D3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/D/D3/a/i}

**False.** Take the bounded <measurable function>
$$
k(x)=\begin{cases}(1+x)^{-1},&x>0,\\0,&x\leq0.\end{cases}
$$
It tends to zero at positive infinity, but
$$
\int_0^R|k(x)|\,dx=\log(1+R)\longrightarrow\infty.
$$
Thus decay to zero alone does not imply <Lebesgue integrability>.

= ii
{parent=2011/D/D3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/D/D3/a/ii}

**False.** Let
$$
k=\mathbf1_{\bigcup_{n\geq1}[n,n+2^{-n-2}]}.
$$
This is a bounded <measurable function>, and the intervals are disjoint, so
$$
\int_0^\infty|k(x)|\,dx=\sum_{n\geq1}2^{-n-2}<\infty.
$$
Nevertheless $k(n)=1$ for every positive <integer> $n$, while $k(n+1/2)=0$. Hence it has no limit at infinity. Narrow spikes separate <Lebesgue integrability> from pointwise decay.

= b
{parent=2011/D/D3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/D/D3/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/D/D3/b/i}

Set $H=\int_0^\pi|h(t)|\,dt<\infty$. By <periodic function>[periodicity] and <countable additivity> of the <Lebesgue integral> of a nonnegative function,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_0^\infty e^{-\beta x}|h(x)|\,dx
&=\sum_{k=0}^\infty e^{-\beta k\pi}
\int_0^\pi e^{-\beta t}|h(t)|\,dt\\
&\leq H\sum_{k=0}^\infty e^{-\beta k\pi}
=\frac{H}{1-e^{-\beta\pi}}<\infty.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus **$f$ is Lebesgue integrable** for every $\beta>0$.

= ii
{parent=2011/D/D3/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/D/D3/b/ii}

Use the nonnegative-part convention $h=h^+-h^-$, where $h^+=\max(h,0)$ and $h^-=\max(-h,0)$. The zero-mean assumption gives
$$
A=\int_0^\pi h^+=\int_0^\pi h^-.
$$
For $k\geq1$, write the <integral> over the $k$th period as $I_k=P_k-N_k$, where
$$
P_k=\int_0^\pi\frac{h^+(t)}{k\pi+t}\,dt,
\qquad N_k=\int_0^\pi\frac{h^-(t)}{k\pi+t}\,dt.
$$
Both $P_k$ and $N_k$ lie in the <interval> $[A/((k+1)\pi),A/(k\pi)]$. Their difference therefore satisfies the stronger estimate
$$
|I_k|\leq\frac{A}{\pi k(k+1)}\leq\frac{A}{\pi k^2}.
$$
Consequently $\sum_{k\geq1}I_k$ converges absolutely as a <series> of numbers, proving the requested limit of integrals over whole periods. This does not assert absolute integrability of the original function: the cancellation took place inside each period.

Indeed, setting $B=\int_0^\pi|h(t)|\,dt$, we have
$$
\int_{k\pi}^{(k+1)\pi}|g(x)|\,dx
\geq\frac{B}{(k+1)\pi}.
$$
The <harmonic series> diverges, so $g$ is <Lebesgue integrability>[Lebesgue integrable] only if $B=0$. Conversely $B=0$ means $h=0$ almost everywhere on one period, and hence on all periods, so $g=0$ almost everywhere. Therefore
$$
\boxed{g\in L^1(\pi,\infty)\iff h=0\text{ almost everywhere}.}
$$
This is an example of <integrating a mean-zero periodic function divided by x>.

= D4
{parent=2011/D}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/D/D4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/D/D4/a}

The <monotone convergence theorem> states that if $f_n$ are nonnegative <measurable function>[measurable functions] on a <measure space> and $f_n\uparrow f$ almost everywhere, then
$$
\boxed{\int f\,d\mu=\lim_{n\to\infty}\int f_n\,d\mu.}
$$
Either side is allowed to be $+\infty$; no integrable dominating function is required.

= b
{parent=2011/D/D4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/D/D4/b}

The partial sums $s_n=\sum_{k=1}^n a_k$ are nonnegative <measurable function>[measurable functions] and increase to an extended-valued function $s$. By the <monotone convergence theorem>,
$$
\int_{\mathbb R}s(x)\,dx
=\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^n\int_{\mathbb R}a_k(x)\,dx
=\sum_{k=1}^\infty\int_{\mathbb R}a_k(x)\,dx<\infty.
$$
A nonnegative function with finite <Lebesgue integral> cannot equal $+\infty$ on a set of positive measure: otherwise its <integral> would exceed $M$ times that measure for every $M>0$. Thus the <series> converges to a finite value almost everywhere. Redefine it to be zero on the exceptional <null set> if a finite-valued representative is desired. The resulting function is <Lebesgue integrability>[Lebesgue integrable], with
$$
\boxed{\int\sum_{k=1}^\infty a_k=\sum_{k=1}^\infty\int a_k.}
$$

= c
{parent=2011/D/D4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/D/D4/c}

Put $x=e^{-t}$. Then
$$
\int_0^1(x\log x)^k\,dx
=(-1)^k\int_0^\infty t^k e^{-(k+1)t}\,dt
=\boxed{\frac{(-1)^k k!}{(k+1)^{k+1}}},
$$
where the second substitution is $u=(k+1)t$ and the final <integral> is the given <gamma function> value. The formula also holds for $k=0$, with value $1$.

For $0<x<1$, the <exponential function> expansion gives
$$
x^{-x}=e^{-x\log x}=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{(-x\log x)^k}{k!}.
$$
Every term is nonnegative. By the <monotone convergence theorem>, integration can be interchanged with summation, and hence
$$
\int_0^1x^{-x}\,dx
=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac1{(k+1)^{k+1}}
=\boxed{\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac1{j^j}}.
$$
This identity is one form of <Sophomore's dream>.

= d
{parent=2011/D/D4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/D/D4/d}

Expand $x^x=e^{x\log x}$. The absolute values of all terms have total <integral>
$$
\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac1{k!}\int_0^1|x\log x|^k\,dx
=\sum_{j=1}^\infty j^{-j}<\infty.
$$
The <series> converges, for instance because $j^{-j}\leq2^{-j}$ for $j\geq2$. Part (b) applied to the absolute values, followed by subtraction of positive and negative parts, therefore justifies termwise integration. It gives the other <Sophomore's dream> identity:
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1x^x\,dx=\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^{j-1}}{j^j}.}
$$
Equivalently, the partial sums are dominated by $e^{|x\log x|}\leq e^{1/e}$, so the <dominated convergence theorem> gives the same justification.

= E
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Topology}

= E3
{parent=2011/E}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2011/E/E3}

A <connected space> has no expression as the union of two disjoint nonempty <open set>[open subsets]. A <continuous function>[continuous] <surjection> to the two-point <discrete topology>[discrete space] would pull back its singleton sets to such a separation. Conversely, a separation $X=U\sqcup V$ defines a <continuous function>[continuous] <surjection> by taking the values $0$ on $U$ and $1$ on $V$. Thus these two conditions are equivalent, including for the empty space.

If a subset $S\subseteq\mathbb R$ contains $a<c$ but omits some $b$ between them, its intersections with $(-\infty,b)$ and $(b,\infty)$ separate it. Hence every nonempty <connected space>[connected] subset is an <interval>. Conversely, let an <interval> $I$ have a separation and choose $a<b$ in its opposite sides. Restrict the associated two-valued <continuous function> to $[a,b]\subseteq I$ and regard it as a real-valued function. The <intermediate value theorem> forces the value $1/2$, a contradiction. Singletons and the empty set are <connected space>[connected] directly.

For the union assertion, let $u:X\to\{0,1\}$ be <continuous function>[continuous]. Each restriction $u|_{A_i}$ is constant because $A_i$ is <connected space>[connected]. Since every pair of the $A_i$ intersects, these constants agree, so $u$ is constant on $X$. There is no <continuous function>[continuous] <surjection>, and $X$ is <connected space>[connected]. The empty indexing case gives $X=\varnothing$ and is harmless.

To apply this to the plane, for each $t\in\mathbb R$ take the union of the horizontal line at height $t$ with the vertical axis. Its two lines are <connected space>[connected] and intersect; all such unions share the vertical axis and cover $\mathbb R^2$. Thus the plane is <connected space>[connected]. For the punctured plane, take, for every $r>0$, the circle of radius $r$ together with the positive horizontal ray. The circle is the <continuous image of a connected space>[continuous image] of $[0,2\pi]$, and intersects that <connected space>[connected] ray. These unions share the ray and cover $\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}$, so the punctured plane is <connected space>[connected] too. Translation gives the same conclusion after removing any one point.

Finally, suppose $g:\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R$ were a <continuous function>[continuous] <injection>. Its image $I$ is a <connected space>[connected] <interval> with at least three points. Choose an interior point $c$ of $I$ and its unique preimage $p$. The restriction of $g$ to $\mathbb R^2\setminus\{p\}$ is a <continuous function>[continuous] <surjection> onto $I\setminus\{c\}$. Its domain is <connected space>[connected], whereas its image is separated by the two half-lines on either side of $c$. This contradicts preservation of <connected space>[connectedness] under <continuous image of a connected space>[continuous images]. Therefore **no continuous <injection> of the plane into the line exists**.

= E4
{parent=2011/E}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2011/E/E4}

A <metric space> is <sequential compactness>[sequentially compact] if every sequence has a <convergent sequence>[convergent] <subsequence> whose limit belongs to the space. A <limit point> $\ell$ of $S$ is a point for which every open ball about $\ell$ contains a point of $S$ other than $\ell$.

Let $(x_n)$ lie in a <compact space>[compact] <metric space>. If one value occurs infinitely often, take a constant <subsequence>. Otherwise its set of values $S$ is infinite. If $S$ had no <limit point>, every point of the space would have a neighborhood containing at most one member of $S$. A finite subcover of these neighborhoods would force $S$ finite, a contradiction. Thus $S$ has a <limit point>; the stated <subsequence> result supplies a <convergent sequence>[convergent] <subsequence>. This proves <sequential compactness>.

Assume now that $X$ is nonempty, as is necessary for the requested minimum and <fixed point>. The function $r(x)=d(f(x),x)$ is <continuous function>[continuous], as a <function composition>[composition] of $x\mapsto(f(x),x)$ with the <metric>. The <extreme value theorem> on a <compact space> implies that $r$ attains its infimum, say at $p$. If $f(p)\neq p$, strict decrease of distances gives
$$
r(f(p))=d(f^2(p),f(p))<d(f(p),p)=r(p),
$$
contradicting minimality. Thus $f(p)=p$. If $p,q$ were distinct <fixed point>[fixed points], then $d(p,q)=d(f(p),f(q))<d(p,q)$, another contradiction. Hence **there is exactly one <fixed point>**. This is the <fixed point theorem for a strictly distance-decreasing map on a compact metric space>.

<Compactness> is essential. On $\mathbb R$, take
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\log(1+e^x).}
$$
Here $f(x)-x=\log(1+e^{-x})>0$, so there is no <fixed point>. Nevertheless $0<f'(x)=e^x/(1+e^x)<1$ everywhere, and the <mean value theorem> gives $|f(x)-f(y)|<|x-y|$ for distinct $x,y$. There is no uniform <Lipschitz continuity>[Lipschitz] constant less than $1$, since $f'(x)\to1$ as $x\to\infty$.

= F
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Multivariable Calculus}

= F2
{parent=2011/F}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/F/F2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/F/F2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/F/F2/a/i}

Writing $f=(f_1,\ldots,f_m)$, the $j$th <partial derivative> at $x$ is the vector limit
$$
\partial_j f(x)=\lim_{t\to0}\frac{f(x+te_j)-f(x)}t,
$$
when it exists. The <total derivative> is a <linear map> $Df(x):\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m$ satisfying
$$
f(x+h)=f(x)+Df(x)h+o(\|h\|).
$$
The <Jacobian matrix> has entries $J_f(x)_{ij}=\partial_j f_i(x)$. If the <total derivative> exists, its matrix in the standard <basis>[bases] is precisely $J_f(x)$, because substitution of $h=te_j$ identifies every column. Existence of the <partial derivative>[partial derivatives] alone does not in general imply existence of the <total derivative>.

= ii
{parent=2011/F/F2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/F/F2/a/ii}

For a scalar-valued differentiable function, its <gradient> is the column vector
$$
\nabla f(x)=\begin{pmatrix}\partial_1f(x)\\\vdots\\\partial_nf(x)\end{pmatrix}.
$$
It represents the <total derivative> by the <Euclidean inner product>:
$$
\boxed{Df(x)h=\nabla f(x)\cdot h.}
$$
Thus $J_f(x)=\nabla f(x)^T$ is a row matrix; the <gradient> and the <total derivative> represent the same first-order information but have different types.

= iii
{parent=2011/F/F2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/F/F2/a/iii}

The <chain rule> states that $f\circ g$ is differentiable at $x$ and
$$
\boxed{D(f\circ g)(x)=Df(g(x))\circ Dg(x).}
$$
In terms of <Jacobian matrix>[Jacobian matrices],
$$
\boxed{J_{f\circ g}(x)=J_f(g(x))J_g(x).}
$$
The factors have dimensions $k\times m$ and $m\times n$, respectively, giving the required $k\times n$ <Jacobian matrix>.

= b
{parent=2011/F/F2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/F/F2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/F/F2/b/i}

Differentiating the reciprocal of $1+x^2+y^2$ gives the <gradient>
$$
\boxed{\nabla f(x,y)=\frac{-2}{(1+x^2+y^2)^2}\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=2011/F/F2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/F/F2/b/ii}

At $(-1,0)$ the <gradient> is $(1/2,0)^T$. Therefore the <total derivative> is the <linear map>
$$
\boxed{Df(-1,0)(h,k)=\frac h2,}
$$
represented by the <Jacobian matrix> $\begin{pmatrix}1/2&0\end{pmatrix}$.

= iii
{parent=2011/F/F2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/F/F2/b/iii}

The <total derivative> of $g$ sends a scalar increment $s$ to
$$
Dg(t)s=s\begin{pmatrix}\cos t\\-\sin t\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Since $\sin^2t+\cos^2t=1$, the <function composition> $f\circ g$ is constantly $1/2$, so $D(f\circ g)(t)=0$. Directly, the <Jacobian matrix> product is
$$
J_f(g(t))J_g(t)=
\begin{pmatrix}-\sin t/2&-\cos t/2\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\cos t\\-\sin t\end{pmatrix}=0.
$$
This verifies the <chain rule> at every $t$.

= iv
{parent=2011/F/F2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/F/F2/b/iv}

The <partial derivative>[partial derivatives] satisfy a global bound of $1$ in absolute value. For example,
$$
|\partial_xf(x,y)|\leq\frac{2|x|}{(1+x^2)^2}\leq1,
$$
because $2|x|\leq1+x^2$ and $1+x^2\geq1$; the same argument applies to $\partial_y f$. Apply the one-dimensional <mean value theorem> first along a horizontal segment and then along a vertical segment:
$$
\begin{aligned}
|f(x,y)-f(u,v)|
&\leq|f(x,y)-f(u,y)|+|f(u,y)-f(u,v)|\\
&\leq\boxed{|x-u|+|y-v|}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus $f$ is <Lipschitz continuity>[Lipschitz] with constant $1$ for the coordinate-sum <norm>.

= G
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Calculus of Variations}

= G2
{parent=2011/G}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2011/G/G2}

For a regular <isoperimetric constraint>, introduce a constant <Lagrange multiplier> $\lambda$ and set $L=F-\lambda G$. With fixed endpoint values, the <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\boxed{L_y-\frac d{dx}L_{y'}=0.}
$$
If $F$ and $G$ have no explicit $x$ dependence, the <Beltrami identity> gives the <first integral>
$$
L-y'L_{y'}=\text{constant}.
$$
The regularity assumption excludes the exceptional case in which the constraint itself has zero <first variation> for every admissible perturbation.

Parametrize the <surface of revolution> by $r(z,\theta)=(f(z)\cos\theta,f(z)\sin\theta,z)$. The magnitude of $r_z\times r_\theta$ is $f\sqrt{1+f'^2}$. Integrating the resulting <surface area> element over $\theta$, and integrating the circular cross-sectional areas for the <volume>, gives
$$
A=2\pi\int_a^b f\sqrt{1+f'^2}\,dz,
\qquad V=\pi\int_a^b f^2\,dz.
$$
Here $f\geq0$ because it is a radius. For a regular connected closed surface, take $f>0$ in the interior; the closing endpoints can have unbounded $f'$ even when the surface itself is smooth.

After dividing $A-\lambda V$ by $\pi$, its integrand is $L=2f\sqrt{1+f'^2}-\lambda f^2$. The <Euler-Lagrange equation> and <Beltrami identity> become
$$
\sqrt{1+f'^2}-\lambda f-
\frac d{dz}\left(\frac{ff'}{\sqrt{1+f'^2}}\right)=0,
\qquad
\frac{2f}{\sqrt{1+f'^2}}-\lambda f^2=C.
$$
As $f$ tends to zero at an endpoint, $0\leq2f/\sqrt{1+f'^2}\leq2f$ also tends to zero, so $C=0$. On the positive interior this implies $\lambda>0$ and, writing $R=2/\lambda$,
$$
1+f'^2=\frac{R^2}{f^2}.
$$
The <Euler-Lagrange equation> also yields $f''=-R^2/f^3$. Therefore
$$
(f^2)''=2(f'^2+ff'')=-2,
$$
including at the maximum where $f'=0$. Integrating and using the <first integral> gives
$$
f(z)^2=R^2-(z-z_0)^2.
$$
Thus the regular extremal is a <sphere>. The two zeros fix its center and radius:
$$
\boxed{z_0=\frac{a+b}2,\qquad R=\frac{b-a}2,\qquad\lambda=\frac2R=\frac4{b-a}.}
$$
Equivalently, the prescribed <volume> fixes $R=(3V/(4\pi))^{1/3}$. If $a,b$ are fixed in advance, their separation and the prescribed <volume> must be compatible with these equations; there is no positive regular stationary profile of the stated type for an incompatible <volume>.

For minimality, the three-dimensional <isoperimetric inequality> states that every sufficiently regular closed surface enclosing <volume> $V$ has
$$
A^3\geq36\pi V^2,
$$
with equality precisely for a <sphere>. Our extremal has $A=4\pi R^2$ and $V=4\pi R^3/3$, so it attains equality and is a **global minimum of area at the fixed <volume>**. This also makes clear the connected-surface assumption behind uniqueness: allowing several disconnected components introduces stationary collections of spheres, rather than the single positive generating profile used here.

= H
{parent=2011}
{scope}
