{title2=Classical Mechanics}

= H2
{parent=2011/H}
{scope}
{c}

Oxford advertises worked solutions to this exact question for its students in https://users.ox.ac.uk/~math0391/CMrevision.pdf[the Classical Mechanics revision notes]. This question is intentionally omitted from the newly authored solutions.

= J
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Quantum Theory}

= J2
{parent=2011/J}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2011/J/J2}

The stationary <Schrödinger equation> is
$$
-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\Delta\psi+V\psi=E\psi.
$$
The <Laplacian> represents the kinetic-<energy> operator. For a <spherically symmetric function>, $\Delta\psi=\psi''+2\psi'/r$. The attractive <Coulomb potential> of the nucleus is $V=-q^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0r)$; $E$ is the <energy> <eigenvalue>. Multiplication by $-2m/\hbar^2$ produces the displayed radial equation with <Bohr radius>
$$
\boxed{a=\frac{4\pi\epsilon_0\hbar^2}{mq^2}.}
$$
For a normalizable bound state, write $\kappa^2=-2mE/\hbar^2$ and take $\lambda>0$. Substitution of $\psi=(A+Br)e^{-\lambda r}$ in
$$
\psi''+\frac2r\psi'+\left(\frac2{ar}-\kappa^2\right)\psi=0
$$
gives, after division by the exponential, the coefficients
$$
\begin{array}{c|c}
r&(\lambda^2-\kappa^2)B\\
1&(\lambda^2-\kappa^2)A-4\lambda B+2B/a\\
1/r&2B-2\lambda A+2A/a.
\end{array}
$$
If $B=0$ and $A\neq0$, these force $\lambda=1/a$ and $\kappa=\lambda$. If $B\neq0$, the first coefficient gives $\kappa^2=\lambda^2$, the second gives $\lambda=1/(2a)$, and the third gives $B=-A/(2a)$. Up to nonzero scalar factors, the two radial bound states and their energies are consequently
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\psi_1(r)&=e^{-r/a},& E_1&=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2ma^2},\\
\psi_2(r)&=(r-2a)e^{-r/(2a)},&E_2&=-\frac{\hbar^2}{8ma^2}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
They are linearly independent as functions and correspond to different <energy> <eigenvalue>[eigenvalues]. Strictly, they are not two solutions of one fixed-<energy> differential equation: each is a solution at its own <energy>.

For the higher-<energy> state, the spherical <Born rule> includes the radial factor $4\pi r^2$. Its normalization constant and the angular factor cancel in the ratio
$$
P(r<a)=\frac{\int_0^a r^2(r-2a)^2e^{-r/a}\,dr}
{\int_0^\infty r^2(r-2a)^2e^{-r/a}\,dr}.
$$
The given <integral> formula makes the denominator $8a^5$ and the numerator $8a^5-21a^5/e$. Thus
$$
\boxed{P(r<a)=1-\frac{21}{8e}.}
$$

= K
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics and Waves}

= K3
{parent=2011/K}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2011/K/K3}

On $\zeta=ae^{i\theta}$, the <Joukowski map> gives $z=2a\cos\theta$, so the image is the segment $S=[-2a,2a]$. Solving the resulting quadratic gives the inverse candidates
$$
\zeta=\frac12\left(z\pm\sqrt{z^2-4a^2}\right).
$$
On $D=\mathbb C\setminus[-2a,2a]$, choose the analytic square <root> $s(z)$ of $z^2-4a^2$ characterized by $s(z)/z\to1$ at infinity. One explicit definition is
$$
s(z)=(z+2a)\sqrt{\frac{z-2a}{z+2a}},
$$
where the square <root> on the right is the <principal square root>. Its argument never lies on the nonpositive real axis for $z\in D$, so this defines the required <holomorphic function>. Then $g(z)=(z+s(z))/2\sim z$.

The two quadratic roots have product $a^2$. For $z\notin S$, neither <root> lies on $|\zeta|=a$, since that circle maps into $S$. Consequently exactly one <root> lies outside the circle, and it is the branch $g$ chosen at infinity. Also, if two exterior points have the same image, then
$$
(\zeta_1-\zeta_2)\left(1-\frac{a^2}{\zeta_1\zeta_2}\right)=0,
$$
whose second factor cannot vanish. Thus the <Joukowski map> is a <conformal map> from $|\zeta|>a$ onto $D$, and $g$ is <conformal map>[conformal] throughout $D$. The two endpoints are branch points and are excluded.

The <complex potential of a two-dimensional potential flow>[complex potential] in the circle plane is the sum of the <uniform flow> term $Ue^{-i\alpha}\zeta$, its circle-image term $Ua^2e^{i\alpha}/\zeta$, and the <point vortex> term $-i\Gamma\log\zeta/(2\pi)$. On $\zeta=ae^{i\theta}$, the first two sum to $2Ua\cos(\theta-\alpha)$, which is real, while the imaginary part of the vortex term is the constant $-\Gamma\log a/(2\pi)$. Hence the circle is a streamline, giving no normal flow. At infinity the first term gives the required <uniform flow>; the logarithmic term supplies circulation $\Gamma$. The potential may be multivalued by a real constant, but its velocity is single-valued.

Conformal transport therefore gives
$$
\boxed{w(z)=U\left(g(z)e^{-i\alpha}+\frac{a^2e^{i\alpha}}{g(z)}\right)
-\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}\log g(z).}
$$
In the frame where the plate is horizontal, its length is $4a$ and the stream has angle $\alpha$; rotating the frame gives the equivalent incidence description. The <complex velocity> is
$$
\frac{dw}{dz}=\frac{W'(\zeta)}{1-a^2/\zeta^2}.
$$
At the trailing edge $z=2a$, corresponding to $\zeta=a$, the denominator has a simple zero. Finite velocity requires
$$
0=W'(a)=-2iU\sin\alpha-\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi a},
$$
so the <Kutta condition> is
$$
\boxed{\Gamma=-4\pi Ua\sin\alpha.}
$$
With this choice, cancellation of the common zero gives
$$
\left.\frac{dw}{dz}\right|_{z=2a}
=\frac{W''(a)}{2/a}
=Ue^{i\alpha}+\frac{i\Gamma}{4\pi a}
=\boxed{U\cos\alpha}.
$$
Thus the limiting velocity has zero transverse component and is tangent to the plate.

= K4
{parent=2011/K}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2011/K/K4}

The <free surface> is material, so applying the material <derivative> to $y-\eta(x,t)$ gives the exact <kinematic boundary condition>
$$
\boxed{\eta_t+\phi_x\eta_x=\phi_y\quad\text{at }y=\eta.}
$$
For an <incompressible flow>[incompressible fluid] with constant density, the <Euler equations> are
$$
\partial_t\boldsymbol u+(\boldsymbol u\cdot\nabla)\boldsymbol u
=-\rho^{-1}\nabla p-g\boldsymbol e_y.
$$
Since the flow is <irrotational flow>[irrotational], $\boldsymbol u=\nabla\phi$ and $(\boldsymbol u\cdot\nabla)\boldsymbol u=\nabla(|\nabla\phi|^2/2)$. Integrating the resulting <gradient> equation gives a function of time on the right of <Bernoulli equation>. Adding a time-dependent constant to $\phi$ removes that function, without changing velocity, and choosing the atmospheric-pressure reference gives
$$
\phi_t+\frac12|\nabla\phi|^2+gy+\frac{p-p_a}\rho=0.
$$
The pressure immediately above the surface is atmospheric; the liquid-side pressure includes the stated <surface tension> jump.

At first order in wave amplitude, the products $\phi_x\eta_x$ and $|\nabla\phi|^2$, the slope-squared correction to curvature, and displacement corrections in evaluating $\phi$ at $y=\eta$ are all higher order. Hence, on $y=0$,
$$
\phi_y=\eta_t,\qquad
\phi_t+g\eta-\frac T\rho\eta_{xx}=0.
$$
For $\eta=A\cos(kx-\omega t)$, a harmonic <velocity potential> decaying as $y\to-\infty$ and satisfying the first condition is
$$
\phi=\frac{A\omega}{k}e^{ky}\sin(kx-\omega t).
$$
Indeed $\Delta\phi=0$ and $\phi_y|_0=A\omega\sin(kx-\omega t)=\eta_t$. Substituting in the second condition yields the <dispersion relation> for a <gravity-capillary wave>:
$$
\boxed{\omega^2=gk+\frac T\rho k^3.}
$$
Taking the positive-frequency branch, the <phase velocity> and <group velocity> are
$$
c=\frac\omega k=\sqrt{\frac gk+\frac T\rho k},
\qquad
c_g=\frac{d\omega}{dk}=\frac{g+3Tk^2/\rho}{2\omega}.
$$
Subtracting gives
$$
c_g-c=\frac{Tk^2/\rho-g}{2\omega}.
$$
Since $\omega>0$,
$$
\boxed{c_g>c\iff k>\sqrt{\rho g/T}.}
$$
The threshold is also the wavenumber at the <minimum phase speed of a gravity-capillary wave>.

\Image[2011-capillary-gravity-speeds.png]
{title=Phase and group speeds of deep-water gravity-capillary waves}
{description=The curves meet at dimensionless wavenumber one, where the phase speed is minimal.}

= M
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Probability}

= M4
{parent=2011/M}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/M/M4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/M/M4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/a/i}

The <moment-generating function> is $M_X(t)=\mathbb E(e^{tX})$ at real $t$ for which this <expectation> is finite. The <characteristic function> is
$$
\boxed{\varphi_X(t)=\mathbb E(e^{itX}),\qquad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Unlike the <moment-generating function>, the <characteristic function> always exists, because $|e^{itX}|=1$.

= ii
{parent=2011/M/M4/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/a/ii}

Use the <characteristic function> to establish <convergence in distribution> by the <Lévy continuity theorem>, especially when exponential moments do not exist; a <Cauchy distribution>, for example, has a <characteristic function> but no finite <moment-generating function> on a neighborhood of zero.

Use the <moment-generating function> for an exponential-tail estimate: for $t>0$ with $M_X(t)<\infty$, <Markov inequality> gives
$$
P(X\geq x)\leq e^{-tx}M_X(t).
$$
Optimizing over $t$ produces a <Chernoff bound>. The modulus-one <characteristic function> does not directly give this positive exponential bound.

= b
{parent=2011/M/M4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/M/M4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/b/i}

The <binomial series> gives, for $|z|<14/5$,
$$
(14-5z)^{-1/2}
=\frac1{\sqrt{14}}\sum_{n=0}^\infty
\frac{\binom{2n}n}{4^n}\left(\frac5{14}\right)^n z^n.
$$
All coefficients are nonnegative, and $g(1)=2/3+1/3=1$. Thus define
$$
P(T=0)=\frac23+\frac1{\sqrt{14}},\qquad
P(T=n)=\frac1{\sqrt{14}}\binom{2n}n\left(\frac5{56}\right)^n\quad(n\geq1).
$$
These numbers sum to $1$, so they define a <probability distribution> on the nonnegative integers with the desired <probability generating function>.

= ii
{parent=2011/M/M4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/b/ii}

Differentiate the <probability generating function>:
$$
g'(z)=\frac52(14-5z)^{-3/2},\qquad
g''(z)=\frac{75}4(14-5z)^{-5/2}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\mathbb ET=g'(1)=\frac5{54},\qquad
\mathbb E[T(T-1)]=g''(1)=\frac{25}{324}.
$$
Using the relation between the second factorial moment and <variance>,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(T)=g''(1)+g'(1)-g'(1)^2
=\frac{235}{1458}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=2011/M/M4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/b/iii}

For a nonnegative <random variable> $Z$ and $u>0$, <Markov inequality> states
$$
\boxed{P(Z\geq u)\leq\frac{\mathbb EZ}{u}.}
$$
It follows by taking expectations in $Z\geq u\mathbf1_{\{Z\geq u\}}$. A finite <expectation> makes the bound informative.

= iv
{parent=2011/M/M4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/b/iv}

Since $2$ lies inside the radius $14/5$ of the <probability generating function>, its nonnegative <series> converges at $2$, and
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E(2^T)=g(2)=\frac23+\frac12=\frac76.}
$$

= v
{parent=2011/M/M4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/b/v}

Apply <Markov inequality> to the nonnegative <random variable> $2^T$. On $\{T\geq5\}$ it is at least $32$, so
$$
\boxed{P(T\geq5)\leq\frac{\mathbb E(2^T)}{32}=\frac7{192}<0.05.}
$$
This exponential bound is substantially stronger here than applying <Markov inequality> directly to $T$.

= c
{parent=2011/M/M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/c}

At a fixed index, <independence> and uniformity give
$$
p=P(X_s=Y_s)=\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\frac1{k^2}=\frac1k.
$$
The match indicators are independent <Bernoulli trial>[Bernoulli trials]. Thus the waiting times between successive matches are independent <geometric distribution>[geometric] variables on $\{1,2,\ldots\}$ with success <probability> $p$. Their <moment-generating function> is
$$
\sum_{r=1}^\infty p(1-p)^{r-1}e^{tr}
=\frac{pe^t}{1-(1-p)e^t},
$$
provided $(1-p)e^t<1$. Since $S_m$ is the sum of $m$ such waiting times,
$$
\boxed{M_{S_m}(t)=\left(\frac{e^t}{k-(k-1)e^t}\right)^m,
\qquad\mathbb ES_m=mk.}
$$
For $k>1$, the finite domain is $t<\log(k/(k-1))$. For $k=1$, every index is a match, so $S_m=m$ deterministically and the <moment-generating function> is $e^{mt}$ for every real $t$.

= d
{parent=2011/M/M4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M4/d}

For $x\geq0$, the <geometric distribution> tail gives
$$
P(T_k>x)=P(S_1>kx)
=\left(1-\frac1k\right)^{\lfloor kx\rfloor}
\longrightarrow e^{-x}.
$$
For $x<0$, $P(T_k\leq x)=0$. The limiting <cumulative distribution function> is therefore $0$ for negative $x$ and $1-e^{-x}$ for nonnegative $x$, and it is continuous everywhere. This proves
$$
\boxed{T_k\xrightarrow{d}\operatorname{Exp}(1),}
$$
with rate parameter $1$ and mean $1$.

= M5
{parent=2011/M}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/M/M5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/M/M5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M5/a/i}

Given $X_n=k>0$, the number remaining next time is uniform on $\{0,\ldots,k-1\}$ and is independent of earlier choices; given $X_n=0$, the next state is $0$. Thus the conditional law of $X_{n+1}$ depends only on $X_n$, which is the <Markov property>.

By contrast, observing only the last number removed generally loses information about the remaining stock. For $N\geq3$, compare the two positive-<probability> histories
$$
(Y_1,Y_2)=(1,1),\qquad (Y_1,Y_2)=(N-1,1).
$$
Both have $Y_2=1$, but the first leaves $N-2>0$ stickers, so $P(Y_3>0\mid Y_1=1,Y_2=1)=1$, whereas the second leaves none, so $P(Y_3>0\mid Y_1=N-1,Y_2=1)=0$. Hence $(Y_n)$ fails the <Markov property>.

The qualification $N\geq3$ is necessary if a <Markov chain> is allowed to have time-dependent transitions. For $N=1$, the removal sequence is the deterministic, time-homogeneous <Markov chain> $1,0,0,\ldots$. For $N=2$, it is a time-inhomogeneous <Markov chain>, although it is not time-homogeneous on its observed removal states: a removal of $1$ at the first step forces another removal of $1$, whereas a removal of $1$ at the second step forces $0$ next. The blanket non-Markov claim should therefore be understood in the generic, nondegenerate setting.

= ii
{parent=2011/M/M5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M5/a/ii}

The <state space> is $\{0,1,\ldots,N\}$, with initial state $X_1=N$. With rows indexed by the current state and columns by the next state, the <transition matrix> is
$$
\boxed{P_{ij}=\begin{cases}
1,&i=j=0,\\
1/i,&1\leq i\leq N,\quad0\leq j<i,\\
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}}
$$
The state strictly decreases at each step until absorption at $0$.

= iii
{parent=2011/M/M5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M5/a/iii}

For a <Markov chain> started at state $i$, let $\tau_i^+=\inf\{n\geq1:X_n=i\}$ denote the first positive return time, using time $0$ for the starting state. A <recurrent state> has $P_i(\tau_i^+<\infty)=1$; a <transient state> has return <probability> strictly less than $1$. An <absorbing state> satisfies $P_{ii}=1$, so the chain remains there forever after arrival and it is necessarily a <recurrent state>.

= iv
{parent=2011/M/M5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M5/a/iv}

State $0$ is an <absorbing state> and a <recurrent state>. From every positive state, the stock decreases strictly at the next step and can never increase, so a return to that positive state is impossible. Thus **all positive states are transient**, with return <probability> zero. Starting from $N$, absorption occurs within at most $N$ sticker-taking children.

= v
{parent=2011/M/M5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M5/a/v}

Let $e_k$ be the expected number of children receiving a sticker when $k$ remain. A first-step decomposition gives
$$
e_0=0,\qquad e_k=1+\frac1k\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}e_j\quad(k\geq1).
$$
Subtracting the equations for $ke_k$ and $(k-1)e_{k-1}$ gives $e_k-e_{k-1}=1/k$. Hence $e_k=H_k$, the $k$th <harmonic number>. In particular
$$
\boxed{e_4=1+\frac12+\frac13+\frac14=\frac{25}{12}.}
$$
Every visit before absorption counts exactly one child who receives at least one sticker.

The same first-step argument applies to the <uniform depletion Markov chain> at every initial stock size.

= vi
{parent=2011/M/M5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M5/a/vi}

After the first child, $X_2$ is uniform on $\{0,\ldots,N-1\}$. Conditional on $X_2=k\geq1$, the mean number removed is $(k+1)/2$; conditional on $X_2=0$, it is zero. The <law of total expectation> therefore gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb EY_2
&=\frac1N\sum_{k=1}^{N-1}\frac{k+1}2\\
&=\boxed{\frac{(N-1)(N+2)}{4N}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For $N=1$, the sum is empty and the same formula gives zero.

= b
{parent=2011/M/M5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2011/M/M5/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M5/b/i}

Use the <state space> $\{1,\ldots,N\}$, since an empty box is refilled immediately. The <probability> that formerly led from $i$ to $0$ now leads to $N$. Thus the <transition matrix> is
$$
\boxed{P_{ij}=\begin{cases}
1/i,&1\leq j<i,\\
1/i,&j=N,\\
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}}
$$
For $i=N$, the second line includes the self-loop obtained when all stickers are taken. When $N=1$, this is simply the one-state <Markov chain>.

= ii
{parent=2011/M/M5/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/M/M5/b/ii}

Let $Q=H_N$ and define
$$
\pi_N=\frac1Q,\qquad
\pi_k=\frac1{(k+1)Q}\quad(1\leq k<N).
$$
The probabilities sum to $Q^{-1}(1+\sum_{k=1}^{N-1}(k+1)^{-1})=1$. For $k<N$, the incoming <probability> is
$$
(\pi P)_k=\sum_{j=k+1}^{N}\frac{\pi_j}{j}
=\frac1Q\left(\sum_{j=k+1}^{N-1}\frac1{j(j+1)}+\frac1N\right)
=\frac1{(k+1)Q}=\pi_k,
$$
using a telescoping sum. Similarly,
$$
(\pi P)_N=\sum_{j=1}^N\frac{\pi_j}{j}
=\frac1Q\left(\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}\frac1{j(j+1)}+\frac1N\right)
=\frac1Q=\pi_N.
$$
Thus $\pi$ is a <stationary distribution>. Every state can reach $N$ in one step, and $N$ can reach every lower positive state in one step, so the chain is <irreducible Markov chain>[irreducible]. The self-loop at $N$ has <probability> $1/N>0$, hence the chain is <aperiodic Markov chain>[aperiodic]. The convergence theorem for a finite irreducible aperiodic <Markov chain> now gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}P(X_n=k)=\pi_k.}
$$
For $N=1$, the conclusion follows directly from the single absorbing/refilled state.

This is the limiting law of the <uniform depletion and refill Markov chain>.

= O
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Statistics}

= O4
{parent=2011/O}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/O/O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/O/O4/a}

Ignoring a factor independent of the parameters, the <likelihood function> is
$$
L(\theta)=\prod_{i,j}\theta_{ij}^{n_{ij}},\qquad
\ell(\theta)=\sum_{i,j}n_{ij}\log\theta_{ij}.
$$
Since every count is positive, a boundary value $\theta_{ij}=0$ has zero likelihood. At an interior optimum, a <Lagrange multiplier> for $\sum\theta_{ij}=1$ gives $n_{ij}/\theta_{ij}=\lambda$. Summing the resulting $\theta_{ij}=n_{ij}/\lambda$ gives $\lambda=N$, so
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{ij}=\frac{n_{ij}}N.}
$$
The log <likelihood function> is strictly concave on the positive simplex, because its <Hessian matrix> is diagonal with entries $-n_{ij}/\theta_{ij}^2<0$. Hence this critical point is the unique global <maximum likelihood estimation>[maximum-likelihood estimate].

= b
{parent=2011/O/O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/O/O4/b}

Under <independence>, the log <likelihood function> separates:
$$
\ell(\alpha,\beta)=\sum_i n_{i.}\log\alpha_i+
\sum_j n_{.j}\log\beta_j.
$$
Maximizing the two terms over their respective <probability> simplices, by the same <Lagrange multiplier> argument as in part (a), gives
$$
\widehat\alpha_i=\frac{n_{i.}}N,\qquad
\widehat\beta_j=\frac{n_{.j}}N.
$$
Therefore the constrained <maximum likelihood estimation>[maximum-likelihood estimates] are
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{ij}^{\,0}=\frac{n_{i.}n_{.j}}{N^2}.}
$$
The associated fitted cell counts are $E_{ij}=N\widehat\theta_{ij}^{\,0}=n_{i.}n_{.j}/N$.

= c
{parent=2011/O/O4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/O/O4/c}

The log <likelihood ratio> statistic comparing the unrestricted fit with the <independence> fit is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Lambda&=2\{\ell(\widehat\theta)-\ell(\widehat\theta^{\,0})\}\\
&=2\sum_{i,j}n_{ij}\log\left(\frac{n_{ij}/N}{n_{i.}n_{.j}/N^2}\right)
=\boxed{2\sum_{i,j}n_{ij}\log\left(\frac{n_{ij}N}{n_{i.}n_{.j}}\right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The unrestricted model has $rc-1=8$ free parameters. <Independence> has $(r-1)+(c-1)=4$, so the difference is $(r-1)(c-1)=4$. By <Wilks theorem>, under the <null hypothesis> the statistic has asymptotically a <chi-squared distribution> with $4$ degrees of freedom, rather than $6$, $8$, or $9$.

For the supplied statistic, the approximate <p-value> is $1-F_4(11.8)=0.02$. Thus **reject <independence> at the 5% level**, but not at the 1% level: the data provide evidence of an association. The approximation is reasonable here; the smallest fitted cell count is $27\cdot99/299\approx8.94$, so all fitted counts exceed $5$. The <statistical hypothesis test> concerns association, not a causal effect of personality.

= O5
{parent=2011/O}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/O/O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/O/O5/a}

The <Gaussian linear model> has log <likelihood function>, up to an additive constant,
$$
\ell(\theta,\sigma^2)=-\frac n2\log\sigma^2-
\frac1{2\sigma^2}(Y-X\theta)^T(Y-X\theta).
$$
For each fixed positive $\sigma^2$, maximize this by minimizing the residual sum of squares. Differentiation gives the <normal equations for linear least squares>[normal equations]
$$
X^TX\widehat\theta=X^TY.
$$
The full column rank of $X$ makes $X^TX$ <positive-definite matrix>[positive definite], hence invertible, so
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY.}
$$
The quadratic objective is strictly convex, which proves uniqueness. The joint <maximum likelihood estimation>[maximum-likelihood estimate] has the same coefficient estimate, except on the <probability>-zero event of an exactly zero residual when the positive-<variance> likelihood has no finite joint maximizer.

= b
{parent=2011/O/O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/O/O5/b}

Write $A=(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$. Since $AX=I_p$,
$$
\widehat\theta=\theta+A\epsilon.
$$
The errors have mean zero and <covariance matrix> $\sigma^2I_n$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E\widehat\theta=\theta,\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(\widehat\theta)=\sigma^2AA^T
=\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}.}
$$
Thus $\widehat\theta$ is an <unbiased estimator>. In addition, the <multivariate normal distribution> is preserved by <linear map>[linear maps], so $\widehat\theta$ is <multivariate normal distribution>[multivariate normal] with the displayed mean and <covariance matrix>.

= c
{parent=2011/O/O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/O/O5/c}

Use the residual mean square
$$
\boxed{s^2=\frac{\|Y-X\widehat\theta\|^2}{n-p}.}
$$
To justify its denominator, let $H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$, the <orthogonal projection matrix> onto the column space of $X$. Then the residual is $(I-H)\epsilon$. The <idempotent matrix> $I-H$ has rank $n-p$, so diagonalizing it with an <orthogonal matrix> gives
$$
\frac{\|Y-X\widehat\theta\|^2}{\sigma^2}\sim\chi^2_{n-p}.
$$
Consequently $\mathbb Es^2=\sigma^2$, making $s^2$ an <unbiased estimator>. If specifically asked for <maximum likelihood estimation>, maximizing over $\sigma^2$ gives the alternative $\widehat\sigma^2_{\rm ML}=\|Y-X\widehat\theta\|^2/n$, which is biased downward by the factor $(n-p)/n$.

= d
{parent=2011/O/O5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/O/O5/d}

Let $v_i=[(X^TX)^{-1}]_{ii}>0$. Part (b) gives
$$
Z_i=\frac{\widehat\theta_i-\theta_i}{\sigma\sqrt{v_i}}\sim N(0,1).
$$
The residual vector and coefficient estimator are independent: they are jointly <multivariate normal distribution>[normal] and their cross-<covariance matrix> is zero, since $X^T(I-H)=0$. Hence $Z_i$ is independent of $U=(n-p)s^2/\sigma^2\sim\chi^2_{n-p}$. By the definition of <Student's t-distribution>,
$$
\frac{\widehat\theta_i-\theta_i}{s\sqrt{v_i}}
=\frac{Z_i}{\sqrt{U/(n-p)}}\sim t_{n-p}.
$$
Let $t_{n-p,1-\alpha/2}$ be the $1-\alpha/2$ quantile of this distribution. Inverting the central <probability> statement gives the exact marginal <confidence interval>
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_i\ \pm\ t_{n-p,1-\alpha/2}\,s\sqrt{[(X^TX)^{-1}]_{ii}}.}
$$
Its coverage is $1-\alpha$ under the stated <Gaussian linear model>; simultaneous coverage for all coefficients would require a separate adjustment.

= P
{parent=2011}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= P3
{parent=2011/P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/P/P3/a}

A <polynomial least-squares approximation> $p_n\in\Pi_n$ minimizes the weighted residual <norm>:
$$
\boxed{\|u-p_n\|=\min_{q\in\Pi_n}\|u-q\|.}
$$
Equivalently it minimizes $\int_{-1}^1x^2(u(x)-q(x))^2\,dx$. This is a best approximation within a finite-dimensional <polynomial> space, without an interpolation requirement.

= b
{parent=2011/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/P/P3/b}

If $p$ is a minimizer, then for each $q\in\Pi_n$ the quadratic
$$
\|u-p-tq\|^2=\|u-p\|^2-2t(u-p,q)+t^2\|q\|^2
$$
has a minimum at $t=0$, so $(u-p,q)=0$. Conversely, if this orthogonality holds, then for every $q\in\Pi_n$ the <Pythagorean theorem> in the <inner product space> gives
$$
\|u-q\|^2=\|u-p\|^2+\|p-q\|^2\geq\|u-p\|^2.
$$
Thus the minimizers are exactly the <orthogonal projection>[orthogonal projections] characterized by
$$
\boxed{(u-p,q)=0\quad\text{for all }q\in\Pi_n.}
$$
For existence, write $p=\sum_{j=0}^n c_jx^j$. The conditions form the <normal equations for linear least squares>[normal equations] $Gc=b$, with <Gram matrix> $G_{ij}=(x^i,x^j)$ and $b_i=(u,x^i)$. For every nonzero coefficient vector $v$,
$$
v^TGv=\int_{-1}^1x^2\left(\sum_jv_jx^j\right)^2\,dx>0.
$$
Indeed a nonzero <polynomial> is nonzero on some open <interval> away from $0$, where the weight is positive. Hence $G$ is a <positive-definite matrix> and is invertible, proving existence and uniqueness of $c$. Uniqueness also follows from the preceding <Pythagorean theorem> identity. The zero of the weight at one point therefore causes no degeneracy on continuous functions or polynomials.

= c
{parent=2011/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/P/P3/c}

Define the reflected <polynomial> $\widetilde p(x)=-p(-x)$. It belongs to $\Pi_n$. Since $u$ is odd, a change of variable and the evenness of the weight give
$$
\|u-\widetilde p\|^2
=\int_{-1}^1x^2(u(x)+p(-x))^2\,dx
=\int_{-1}^1x^2(u(x)-p(x))^2\,dx.
$$
Thus $\widetilde p$ is another <polynomial least-squares approximation>. By uniqueness, $\widetilde p=p$, so
$$
\boxed{p(-x)=-p(x).}
$$
The conclusion includes the zero <polynomial>, which is both odd and even.

= d
{parent=2011/P/P3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/P/P3/d}

By part (c), the best <polynomial> of degree at most $2$ is odd, hence has the form $p_2(x)=cx$. The <orthogonal projection> condition with $q=x$ gives
$$
c=\frac{(x^{2011},x)}{(x,x)}
=\frac{\int_{-1}^1x^{2014}\,dx}{\int_{-1}^1x^4\,dx}
=\frac{2/2015}{2/5}=\frac1{403}.
$$
The residual is odd, so its weighted inner products with the even polynomials $1$ and $x^2$ vanish automatically. Thus all three <normal equations for linear least squares>[normal equations] hold and
$$
\boxed{p_2(x)=\frac{x}{403}.}
$$

= P4
{parent=2011/P}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2011/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/P/P4/a}

For $w\neq0$, the <Householder transformation> is
$$
\boxed{H(w)=I-2\frac{ww^T}{w^Tw}.}
$$
Let $P=ww^T/(w^Tw)$. Then $P^T=P$ and $P^2=P$, since $P$ is the <orthogonal projection matrix> onto the span of $w$. Hence $H^T=H$ and
$$
H^TH=H^2=(I-2P)^2=I-4P+4P^2=I.
$$
Thus $H$ is a <symmetric matrix> and an <orthogonal matrix>. Geometrically it reverses the component parallel to $w$ and fixes the perpendicular <hyperplane>.

= b
{parent=2011/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/P/P4/b}

Let $r=\|u\|_2>0$, and choose $s=1$ if $u_1\geq0$ and $s=-1$ otherwise. Set
$$
w=u+sr e_1.
$$
This vector is nonzero, because $w_1$ has magnitude $|u_1|+r$. Direct computation gives
$$
w^Tu=r(r+su_1),\qquad w^Tw=2r(r+su_1),
$$
so the <Householder transformation> satisfies
$$
\boxed{H(w)u=u-2w\frac{w^Tu}{w^Tw}=u-w=-s\|u\|_2 e_1.}
$$
The first entry is nonzero and every other entry is zero. The sign choice also avoids cancellation when forming the first entry of $w$, which is useful in numerical computation.

= c
{parent=2011/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/P/P4/c}

Apply part (b) to the first column of $A$, using the identity if it is already the zero vector. This gives an <orthogonal matrix> $H_1$ for which $H_1A$ has zeros below its first diagonal entry. At stage $j$, apply a <Householder transformation> to rows $j,\ldots,n$ of column $j$, and extend it by the identity on the first $j-1$ coordinates. If that trailing column is zero, again use the identity. The transformation preserves zeros in earlier columns, since their trailing entries already vanish.

After at most $n-1$ stages,
$$
R=H_{n-1}\cdots H_1A
$$
is an <upper triangular matrix>. Each $H_j$ is symmetric and orthogonal, so with
$$
Q=H_1\cdots H_{n-1}
$$
we have $Q^TQ=I$ and
$$
\boxed{A=QR.}
$$
No invertibility assumption was used, so the <Householder QR decomposition> also exists for singular matrices.

= d
{parent=2011/P/P4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2011/P/P4/d}

Multiply $Ax=b$ by $Q^T$ to get the equivalent triangular system
$$
Rx=Q^Tb.
$$
For nonsingular $A$, compute $c=Q^Tb$ and use <back substitution>:
$$
x_i=\frac{c_i-\sum_{j=i+1}^nR_{ij}x_j}{R_{ii}},\qquad i=n,n-1,\ldots,1.
$$
Orthogonal transformations preserve the <Euclidean norm>, making this a numerically stable reduction. The factors can be applied as stored <Householder transformation>[Householder transformations] without explicitly forming $Q$.

Since $Q$ is invertible, $A$ is singular exactly when $R$ is singular. For an <upper triangular matrix>,
$$
\det R=\prod_{i=1}^nR_{ii}.
$$
Thus **a singular $A$ forces at least one zero diagonal entry in $R$**. In that case, ordinary division-based <back substitution> must be replaced by a consistency/rank analysis: the system may have no solution or infinitely many solutions, and a rank-revealing factorization can identify the free parameters.
